  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThis week into next weekend...\n\nToday through late week (Thursday): Morning surface and GOES East \nwater vapor/RAP analysis indicate post tropical cyclone Isaias over \nnorth-central AL. Cold core aloft over the same area is keeping \nmean northwesterly flow locked in the the west side of the \ndecaying system. Isentropic ascent has kept stratus ongoing in the\nGolden Triangle but this will markedly decrease over the next \nseveral hours. As the upper low and remnant system lift northeast,\ncloud cover will decrease into the overnight hours. Lows will be \nsomewhat less seasonable, some 3-8F above, but slightly more \npleasant (55-62F). Dry afternoon under light westerly winds and \nsunny to mostly sunny skies are on tap today. Highs will moderate\nback up seasonably warm, some 10-15F above (88-94F), with warmest\nareas west of Interstate 55 (91-94F).\n\nContinued warmth into mid October will be the story for the week. \nMean ridge around 594DM will settle in over the northwest Gulf and \nbuild to the northeast into the Gulf Coast states. Dry weather and \nseasonably warm temperatures (highs in 90-96F range and lows mid 50s \nto around 60 degrees in east MS to low to upper 60s in central to \nnorthwest Delta) will be the norm. There could be enough low \nafternoon humidity to support some limited fire danger that will \nhave to be monitored early this week. However, winds remain light\nenough to preclude any mention in HWO for Monday. Low level return\nflow will bring some humidity through the week but nothing on the\norder of dangerous heat stress we have been dealing with for some\ntime.\n\nLate week into next weekend (Friday into Saturday): A more fall-like\nfrontal passage looks to finally be on tap into late week into \nnext weekend. An upper low will remain parked over the Four \nCorners earlier in the week and potentially will interact with a \ndeepening trough/upper jet diving into central Canada and southeast.\nThere is differing solutions of synoptic evolution: one camp of \nthe Euro retrograding the upper low while the core of the trough/jet\ndive into the Great Lakes to northeast while the GFS phase to the\ntwo features and a deep longwave synoptic trough develops east of\nthe MS River corridor. Both bring a stout, dry cold front but the\nGFS carves out the moisture much more significantly (i.e. dewpoints\nfalling into teens to 20s vs mid 40s for the Euro). At the very \nleast, cool down looks on tap into next weekend. Highs will be \nseasonably warm Friday (86-92F), with lows in the low to mid 60s \nthat morning and lows that night mid 50s north of Interstate 20 to\nnear 60 degrees to the south. Seasonable and pleasant fall \ntemperatures will build areawide into next weekend (highs of \n73-80F and lows of upper 40s north of Interstate 20 to low 50s to \nthe south). /DC/",
